1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,619
How does our system talk to Uzcard and Humo, when they speak different languages?

2
00:00:04,619 --> 00:00:09,845
Uzcard speaks REST, and Humo speaks SOAP, with different fields and different errors.

3
00:00:09,845 --> 00:00:15,107
If the payment module knew both, every new scheme would mean touching payment code.

4
00:00:15,107 --> 00:00:23,928
So we define one port, a card processor, with a few commands: one-time code, authorize, capture, reverse, and status.

5
00:00:23,928 --> 00:00:26,500
Each scheme gets its own adapter behind that port.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,309
The payment module sends the same command, whatever the card.

7
00:00:30,309 --> 00:00:36,988
Each adapter translates the scheme's errors into our own codes, so a decline looks the same everywhere.

8
00:00:36,988 --> 00:00:40,821
We demo every command against the schemes' test environments.

9
00:00:40,821 --> 00:00:44,773
Next, knowing a card's scheme, and storing its number safely.

